   "response_text": "All green, woof! \ud83d\udc36\n\nWhat I changed (library source only \u2014 no test files touched):\n\n**`invkit/report.py`**\n- `format_report(items, sort_by=\"sku\", descending=False)` \u2014 new keyword-only-ish param (stays positional-compatible, defaults `False`).\n- Feeds `reverse=descending` straight into `sorted(...)`, so it works uniformly for *every* key in `SORT_KEYS` (`sku`, `name`, `quantity`, `price`, `value`) instead of hand-rolling per-key reversal. DRY wins.\n\n**`invkit/cli.py`**\n- Added `--desc` via `action=\"store_true\"` (so it's flag-only, no value, and `False` by default).\n- Passed it through: `format_report(items, sort_by=args.sort, descending=args.desc)`.\n- Nudged the module docstring to mention `[--desc]`.\n\nVerification:\n- `pytest -q` \u2192 4 passed (untouched suite still happy).\n- Direct API check: `descending=True` reverses on a non-default key (`quantity`).\n- CLI checks: plain invocation is byte-identical to before (default behaviour preserved), `--sort price --desc` reverses, `--desc` alone reverses the default `sku` ordering, and it composes fine with `--low`.\n\nOne tiny call-out (not a blocker): for equal sort keys Python's stable sort preserves input order rather than reversing ties \u2014 standard `reverse=True` semantics, so `descending=True` is exactly the mirror of the ascending comparator, nothing weirder.\n\nDONE",
